                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega = \langle -,[-,-]\rangle
            as above, we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p= 1
            and so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            here is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle 2-bundle with connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle+gerbe"} with connection
            %em WZW gerbe
            or similar.
          %p
            This terminology arises as follows. In (
            %a{:href => "Wess-Zumino-Witten+model#WessZumino71"}> Wess-Zumino 71
            ) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            propagating on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            was considered, with only the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+action"} kinetic action
            term. Then in (
            %a{:href => "Wess-Zumino-Witten+model#Witten84"}> Witten 84
            ) it was observed that for this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            to give a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            after
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction+term"} interaction term
            has to the added. The resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            came to be known as the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+model"} Wess-Zumino-Witten model
            or
            %em WZW model
            for short, and the term that Witten added became the
            = succeed "." do
              %em WZW term
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            it describes the propagation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            on the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            subject to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+form"} Killing form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            and subject to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+field"} higher gauge force
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "Wess-Zumino-Witten+model#Gawedzki87"} Gawedzki 87
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface+holonomy"} surface holonomy
            functional of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle+gerbe"} connection on a bundle gerbe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            that we just motivated above.
          %p
            Later WZW terms, or at least their curvature forms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+sigma-models+for+super+p-branes"} Green-Schwarz sigma-models for super p-branes
            each have an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            that is the sum of the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+action"} kinetic action
            plus a WZW term of degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+2
          %p
            In general WZW terms are “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauged+WZW+model"}> gauged
            ” which means, as we will see, that they are not defined on the give
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-group"} smooth infinity-group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            itself, but on a bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde G
            of differential moduli stacks over that group, such that a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to \tilde G
            is a pair consisting of a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to G
            and of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+field"} higher gauge field
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            (a “tensor multiplet” of fields).
          %h4#FormalizationGenerally Generally
          %p
            The following (
            %a{:href => "#FSS12"}> FSS 12
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
